// logos.std.wql.prepared — THE PLAN AS A RUNTIME VALUE (ADR 0024 S4i).
//
// A query compiles when nothing is known about the data, so what a compile-time
// plan can optimize is bounded; by the time the data exists, the code is fixed
// and the plan cannot be influenced. S4h answered that with two nests and a
// run-time discriminant — but a discriminant re-evaluated on every call is a
// property of the FUNCTION, not an object an application holds. What it should
// be is JDBC's PreparedStatement: the plan is DECIDED once, against
// representative data, and then APPLIED many times.
//
// So each query emits FOUR items instead of one:
//
//   struct <Name>Plan       the decision, plus THE FACTS IT RESTED ON
//   <q>_prepare(sources)    measures, decides once, returns the plan
//   <q>_run(plan, sources)  applies a plan to data — the query's real body
//   <q>(sources)            prepare-then-run, so nothing existing changes
//
// ⚠ THE DATA IS NOT PART OF THE PLAN, which is exactly why a plan can be
// reused: `_run` takes the sources as arguments and the plan holds no reference
// to anything it measured. That also makes "reusable on data of the same
// distribution" a CLAIM BY THE CALLER — and an unchecked claim yields a
// silently pessimal plan. Hence the facts: the plan carries the numbers the
// choice rested on, so re-preparing is a COMPARISON (`agrees`) rather than a
// guess, and a plan can say how close to flipping it is (`margin`). That is
// EXPLAIN addressed to the program rather than to a human — the human's half is
// `why`, the ground recorded at the moment of deciding.
//
// ⚠ ONE PLAN TYPE PER QUERY, not one shared `Plan`. A plan is a decision about a
// specific query's specific sources, so applying one to another query is a type
// error rather than a silently pessimal run — the type system carries the
// association and nothing has to check it.
//
// ⚠ PER-ROW COST IS UNCHANGED. The decision is a field of the plan, read once
// where the size comparison used to be read once; the loop nest below it is
// still the specialised one the compiler emitted.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THE PLAN MAY REST ON is only what `prepare` can measure BEFORE the
// query runs: the parameters, and the sizes sources DECLARE (S4g). A derived
// rel's row count is not such a fact — obtaining it means materializing the
// rel, which is the query's own work — so a join whose sides can only be
// measured that way keeps ONE nest and says so. A prepared plan that had to run
// the query to be prepared would not be a plan.
//
// ── THE SECOND DECISION POINT (ADR 0024 S4j) ─────────────────────────────────
// The paragraph above is where the argument STOPPED, and it named only one of
// the two moments a plan can be decided at:
//
//   (1) `prepare` — before any data is touched. Facts: the parameters and the
//       sizes sources DECLARE. Reusable across calls. This is the
//       PreparedStatement, and nothing about it moves.
//
//   (2) inside `run`, AFTER the prelude's unavoidable materializations and
//       BEFORE the join builds its index. Facts: everything from (1) plus the
//       `len()` of every relation this query materializes ANYWAY. Per-call,
//       therefore not reusable.
//
// A rel's row count is not a fact about the input data — that is why (1) may not
// have it. But by the time the join's index is built, the query has already
// materialized the rel for its own reasons: the binding exists, and its `len()`
// is the same field read the nest's own loop condition makes. So the number that
// (1) could not afford is FREE at (2), and refusing there was refusing for a
// reason that had already stopped applying.
//
// This is a DEFERRED HALF OF THE SAME PLAN, not a second mechanism: one plan
// object, one decision channel, one `why` vocabulary. The plan reports which
// half decided (`dyn_order` vs `defer_order`) and `explain()` says so, so a
// reader can tell (1) from (2) without opening the emitter.
//
// ⚠ WHAT IS STILL REFUSED, and for a reason that does not weaken with time: a
// STREAMED side has no `len()` at (2) either, and obtaining one costs draining
// the iterator — the query's own work traded for a plan fact, which is the exact
// error this whole axis exists to avoid. Such a join keeps one nest at both
// points.
//
// ⚠ AND A DEFERRED DECISION IS NOT A PREPARED ONE. `agrees` and `margin` answer
// about the PREPARED half only: the deferred half is re-derived on every call, so
// there is nothing stale about it to check and nothing to be close to flipping.
// `settled()` is what says so out loud — a caller holding a plan must not believe
// the deferred choice was fixed when they prepared it.
package logos.std.wql.prepared;

use logos.lang.str;      // str_byte_at
use logos.mem.string;    // String

// ── What the emitter carries into the shell ────────────────────────────────
// `on` — emit the prepared surface at all. FALSE for the generated helpers a
// query is made of (a rel's per-relation fn, an SCC's semi-naïve driver): those
// are internals of one query's body, not queries an application holds.
//
// `dynamic` — the plan holds a RUN-TIME discriminant. Where it does not, the
// surface is still emitted and still uniform: the plan reports that it rests on
// nothing measurable, `agrees` is then unconditionally true, and a caller need
// not know which queries have a choice to write code that re-checks them all.
//
// `deferred` — the plan holds the decision's RULE but not its answer: the two
// numbers only exist inside `run` (point 2), so `prepare` records that the order
// is chosen per call and `run` chooses it. `dynamic` and `deferred` are mutually
// exclusive by construction — one decision, taken at one of the two points.
//
// `base_sz` / `step_sz` are the two size EXPRESSIONS. For `dynamic` they are
// evaluable in `prepare`'s scope after `measure`; for `deferred` they are
// evaluable in `run`'s, after the prelude. The comparison over them is made in
// ONE place per point — the emitted `prepare`, or the one deferred binding above
// the nest — so the plan and the branch cannot disagree about it.
pub struct Prepared {
    pub on:       bool,
    pub dynamic:  bool,
    pub deferred: bool,
    pub base_sz:  str,
    pub step_sz:  str,
    pub measure:  str,
    pub why:      str,
}

pub fn prep_off() -> Prepared {
    return Prepared { on: false, dynamic: false, deferred: false, base_sz: "",
                      step_sz: "", measure: "", why: "" };
}

/// A prepared surface with no run-time decision in it — the plan is whatever
/// the compiler already fixed, and `why` says what fixed it.
pub fn prep_fixed(why: str) -> Prepared {
    return Prepared { on: true, dynamic: false, deferred: false, base_sz: "",
                      step_sz: "", measure: "", why: why };
}

pub fn prep_dyn(base_sz: str, step_sz: str, measure: str, why: str) -> Prepared {
    return Prepared { on: true, dynamic: true, deferred: false, base_sz: base_sz,
                      step_sz: step_sz, measure: measure, why: why };
}

/// The DEFERRED half (ADR 0024 S4j): `prepare` measures nothing and decides
/// nothing, and `run` decides once per call from two lengths that cost nothing
/// where it stands. No `measure` — a deferred plan's numbers are not reporter
/// calls, they are the lengths of bindings the prelude already made, which is
/// precisely what makes the deferral affordable.
pub fn prep_deferred(base_sz: str, step_sz: str, why: str) -> Prepared {
    return Prepared { on: true, dynamic: false, deferred: true, base_sz: base_sz,
                      step_sz: step_sz, measure: "", why: why };
}

/// The plan TYPE's name: the query's name in CamelCase, plus `Plan`.
///
/// A type is what the caller writes down (`let p: ByKeyPlan = …`), so it gets a
/// type's spelling rather than the fn's. The transform is the only place the two
/// names are related, so nothing else has to know the convention.
pub fn prep_type_name(fn_name: str, out: &mut String) {
    let mut up: bool = true;
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < fn_name.len() {
        let c: u8 = str_byte_at(fn_name, i);
        if c == 95u8 {
            // `_` is a word boundary in the fn's spelling and nothing in the
            // type's.
            up = true;
        } else {
            if up && c >= 97u8 && c <= 122u8 {
                out.push_u8(c - 32u8);
            } else {
                out.push_u8(c);
            }
            up = false;
        }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    out.push_str("Plan");
}

/// `<fn_name><suffix>`, with the visibility marker (a leading `-`, params.logos)
/// kept in FRONT — the derived fns are as public as the query is, and the shell
/// consumes the marker where it writes the head.
pub fn prep_derived_name(fn_name: str, suffix: str, out: &mut String) {
    out.push_str(fn_name);
    out.push_str(suffix);
}

/// A Logos string LITERAL for `s`. The grounds a plan records are written in
/// this file's own prose and travel into GENERATED SOURCE, where an unescaped
/// quote or backslash would be a syntax error at a position no reader can use.
pub fn push_str_lit(out: &mut String, s: str) {
    out.push_str("\"");
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < s.len() {
        let c: u8 = str_byte_at(s, i);
        if c == 34u8 || c == 92u8 {
            out.push_u8(92u8);
            out.push_u8(c);
        } else if c == 10u8 {
            out.push_u8(92u8);
            out.push_u8(110u8);
        } else {
            out.push_u8(c);
        }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    out.push_str("\"");
}
